\section{Using icon scales}{
 Like \code{\link[leaflet:addLegend]{leaflet::addLegend()}}, a legend can be
generated automatically by passing an icon scale function to \code{pal}
alongside the \code{values} used on the map. The icons and labels are then
determined from the levels or bins of the scale, and labels are formatted
using \code{labFormat}. For example, an \code{\link[=iconBin]{iconBin()}} scale would be shown as one
icon per bin, labelled with the range of that bin.

In this case \code{colors} may also be a colour scale function, such as one
created using \code{\link[leaflet:colorBin]{leaflet::colorBin()}} with the same settings as the icon
scale, giving a legend of matched icon-colour pairs.

Legend items are derived exactly as in \code{\link[leaflet:addLegend]{leaflet::addLegend()}}: factor
scales show the sorted unique \code{values}, bin scales show one item per bin,
and quantile scales show one item per quantile of \code{values} (with the
quantile values in a hover tooltip). An icon legend and a colour legend
built from scales with the same settings will therefore always agree.
Unlike \code{\link[leaflet:addLegend]{leaflet::addLegend()}}, \code{values} may be omitted when the scale has
a fixed \code{domain}/\code{levels}, and \code{\link[=iconBin]{iconBin()}} scales with no \code{domain} are
supported. For the latter, the legend shows only the bins that contain
\code{values}, with the same icons (and colours, if \code{colors} is a colour scale)
as the map. Like \code{\link[leaflet:colorBin]{leaflet::colorBin()}}, such a scale assigns icons based on
which bins are occupied, so empty bins have no icon to show.

If any \code{values} are missing, an extra legend item is added using the
\code{na.icon} of the scale and labelled with \code{na.label}. Its colour is the
\code{na.color} of \code{colors} if it is a colour scale, and otherwise grey
(\code{"#808080"}, the default \code{na.color} in \code{{leaflet}}). As in
\code{\link[leaflet:addLegend]{leaflet::addLegend()}}, this is skipped if \code{na.label} is already one of the
labels, if \code{na.icon} is \code{NA}, or if \code{colors} is a colour scale with a
transparent \code{na.color}.
}

\section{Showing marker shapes}{
 By default, legend items are bare icons
coloured with \code{colors}, which suits maps where colour is applied to the
icons themselves (e.g., \code{magicIcons(marker = "none")}). When \code{markers} is
provided, each item is instead drawn as a small version of the marker used
on the map: the shape is filled with \code{colors} and the icon drawn on top in
\code{iconColors}, exactly as in \code{\link[=magicIcons]{magicIcons()}}.

\code{markers} may be a single shape used for every item, one shape per item,
or an icon scale (e.g., \code{iconFactor(c("circle", "square"), domain)}) whose
values are marker shapes. As with a colour scale in \code{colors}, a marker
scale is applied to the same values as \code{pal}, so the two should share the
same settings.

If the shape of the markers is the only thing mapped to a variable, pass
that scale to \code{markers} and leave \code{pal} out: the legend items are then
derived from the marker scale instead. In this case \code{icons} is optional;
give a single icon to show it in every marker (as on the map), or leave
it out to show the shapes alone.
}

